<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet>
  <title>Reading Quiz 14: Angular Momentum, Torque, and Moment of Inertia</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      <alert>Contents:</alert> This quiz covers sections 7.3 through 7.5. 
    </p>
    <p>
      <alert>Instructions:</alert> Answer all the questions to the best of your ability on paper. When you are done, screenshot your work and upload 
      it as a pdf using the <q>File Upload</q> question below. When you submit the quiz, <alert>don't navigate away from Canvas.</alert> Scroll
      down to the bottom of the page for a download link to the solutions to the quiz.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
          <p>
          		K&amp;K calculate just the Z component of <m>\vec{L}</m> to get around the fact that,
              in general, <m>\vec{L}</m> is not parallel to <m>\vec{\omega}</m>. Show, however,that
              <m>\vec{L}</m> is parallel to <m>\vec{\omega}</m> if the body is rotationally symmetric
              about the direction of <m>\vec{\omega}</m>. 
              The picture has a hint here: the <q>problematic components</q> should cancel in pairs.
          </p>

          <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz14/symmetric-top.svg" width="40%">
            <shortdescription>
              A spinning top in the shape of a solid of revolution turns
              about the revolution axis.
            </shortdescription>
            <description>
              <p>
                A spinning to has the shape of a solid of revolution. The axis of revolution
                is vertical, and the bottom of the top has a sharp tip.
              </p>
              <p>
                The angular velocity vector <m>\vec{\omega}</m> is vertical, and the direction
                of rotation is counterclockwise as seen from above.
              </p>
              <p>
                Two masses symmetrically placed with respect to the axis of revolution are shown,
                one to the right and one to the left of the axis. They have the same mass <m>m</m>
                and move on a common circle of radius <m>r</m>.
              </p>
            </description>
          </image>

      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	If you have a ring and a disk of the same mass and radius rotating about their center, example 7.1ab) 
            shows that <m>I_{\text{ring}} \gt I_{\text{disk}}</m>. How do you explain this intuitively?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          	Show that you can obtain the result of example 7.3d) by applying the parallel axis theorem instead of 
            direct integration, starting from the result of 7.3c)
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rank the moment of inertia of the structure in the figure below about 
          the different axes of rotation shown.
        </p>

        <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz14/moment-of-inertia.svg" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>4 structures shaped like the letter H are shown. They
            rotate about different axes.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A structure is made with two vertical bars linked at their centers by
              a horizontal bar. The bars have the same height. 
              Different axes of rotation for this structure are shown.
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Rotation axis (a) is a horizontal axis going through
                    the bottom of the vertical bars.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Rotation axis (b) is a vertical axis going through the center of one
                    the vertical bars.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Rotation axis (c) is a horizontal axis going through the center of the
                    horizontal bar.
                  </p>
                  <p>
                    Rotation axis (d) is a vertical axis going through the center of the horizontal
                    bar.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </page>

  <page>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          You are using a wrench to loosen a nut. Rank the arrangements used in the figure below from
          least to most effective
        </p>

        <image source="images/quiz-images/quiz14/wrench-try2.svg" width="100%">
          <shortdescription>Four different ways of turning a wrench, with or without the help
            of a rod (cheater bar).
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              The image shows four different ways of turning a wrench using a horizontal force
              <m>\vec{F}</m> which is identical in all four situations.
            </p>
            <p>
              <ul>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Option (a): the wrench is oriented vertically and the force is applied at the
                    edge of the wrench.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Option (b): the wrench is oriented vertically and the rod is attached vertically
                    to the end of the wrench. The force is applied at the top end of the rod.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Option (c): The wrench is oriented at an angle to the vertical, and the force is applied
                    at the edge of the wrench. The force is still horizontal. 
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Option (d): The wrench is oriented vertically, and the rod is attached to it horizontally.
                    The force is applied horizontally at the far end of the rod.
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </statement>
    </exercise>

  </page>
</worksheet>
